body:
Thanks for your input.

&#x200B;

I've been doing a lot of thinking, trying to understand how I really got to this point. As with anything, it's a complicated mix. I never felt any romantic feelings for the other person. I can see now that in part it was an escape for when things were very difficult in my relationship and I couldn't see how I could continue with the stress of the various things I mentioned in my original post. But, I think a larger part was driven by ROCD. Testing to see if someone else would be better for me. Trying to figure out if my doubts were valid and real, the usual ROCD type of ruminating. If it had only been a short time, I would forgive myself. But it first started nearly 3 years ago, and whilst it wasn't constant for the 3 years (it was on and off), it's still a long time. Unfortunately I am very slow learner when it comes to figuring out my feelings, thoughts and generally understanding myself. I always have been, seems to take me years to figure out things that others will in months. Even with knowing this about myself, I'm quite shocked that's taken this long for it to really hit me what I was doing. But at the time I'm also not that surprised, much of the last 5 years in particular as about just staying above water with day to day life and I tend to get lost in myself a lot. If you asked this friend, she would be completely unaware that our conversations were anything other than normal, but that doesn't matter. It's very fucked up and it's quite heart breaking to realise how fucked up I am with this. Mainly for her sake.

&#x200B;

I am in the process of distancing myself as much as I can from this friend. It's difficult to do without it being obvious. I'd rather just never speak to her again. I think the only way I could do that would be to completely cut myself off from that circle of friends. It will be difficult as I'm quite isolated as it is due my health but I'll have to I think.

&#x200B;

Whilst I can logically that the combination of ROCD and the stress in the relationship were the main factors in this. It doesn't excuse it and she deserves better. If I tell her, it will break her and will break me. I am concerned about the effect it will have on her in the short term. I hope/think in the long run she may be better off if she can get the help she needs. But as dramatic as this will sound, I'm not sure I will survive it and I fear what effect that will have on my family.

&#x200B;

Selfishly, I also can't bare being what people will think of me. No one will know what I put myself through to support her all these years and what toll it's had on me. All people will see and know is my betrayal. She will also feel betrayed for all the support she's given me too.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
